#1  Polynomials
===============================================================================================

  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial                   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elementary_arithmetic
  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Associative_property         https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commutative_property
  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Distributive_property

  A polynomial is an exhaustive set of equivalent polynomial expressions.
  A polynomial expression is an arithmetical expression involving two types of terms: constants and variables.
  An arithmetical expression is a mathematical expression involving only addition and multiplication (sums and products).
  An addition is sometimes called a subtraction (difference) -- usually when involving one or several negative numbers.
  A multiplication is sometimes called a division (ratio) -- usually when involving one or several rational numbers.
  The notation of a repeated multiplication of the same term by itself can be simplified with exponentiation of said term
  to an integer power.
  
  Because of this property, classical definitions often present exponentiation as a third type of operation
  and exponents as a third type of term.
  This is valid for polynomials as long as the exponents are integers.
  That is: a polynomial can feature exponentiation if and only if said exponentiation is in fact a product.
  
  Choose the less confusing formulation of the definition.
  I like mine better hopefully, but perhaps I'm weird.

  A polynomial can be defined by several distinct (but equal) expressions.
  One of them is called canonical.
  It's obtained by making use of commutativity, associativity and distributivity so that each instance of a variable
  (or product of variables if more than one variable) with the same exponent(s) appears only once, therefore having a
  single constant factor.
  E.g.:    ax² + bx²  =  (a + b) x²
  This single constant factor is called a coefficient.
  The highest integer exponent -- whose corresponding coefficient is *non-zero* -- is called degree of the polynomial.
  The coefficient associated to this exponent is called the leading coefficient.

    For instance,  y = x  is a special case of the affine function  y  =  a1 * x^1  +  a0 * x^0
    which is a polynomial of order 1 with coefficients  a1 = 1  and  a0 = 0.
  
    Or:  y = 1.33  is equivalent to  y  =  1.33 * x^0
    which is a polynomial of order 0 whose unique coefficient is a0 = 1.33.
  
    And of course:  y  =  a2 * x^2  +  a1 * x  +  a0  is a polynomial of order 2 -- as long as a2 is non-zero.

  A polynomial with strictly real coefficients *in canonical form* is called a real polynomial.
  If it has complex coefficients, it's a complex polynomial.
  If it has complex coefficient but is not in canonical form, we cannot say whether it's complex or real.
  Important distinction: "real" in "real polynomial" only concerns the coefficients -- the variable can still be complex.
  
  A polynomial of negative order is called a Laurent polynomial, after Pierre Alphonse Laurent.
  
  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laurent_polynomial
  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pierre_Alphonse_Laurent
  
  Since  x^-n  =  1 / x^n  a Laurent polynomial is not defined at x = 0.
  We won't be needing Laurent polynomials here. You'll see why later during this chapter.

  We have a definition. Let's build stuff.

  //  Order 0 "constant"

  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constant_function

  ƒ (x)  =  a0

  Then  ƒ (x)  =  0  means  a0 = 0  which has 1 solution for a0 but none for x.
  Therefore a polynomial of the zeroth order has zero root.

  //  Order 1 "affine" or "linear"

  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linearity#Linear_polynomials
  
  ƒ (x)  =  a1 x  +  a0
  
  ƒ (x)  =  0    <=>    a1 x  +  a0    <=>    a1 (x  +  a0/a1)  =  0    <=>    x  =  -a0/a1

  /*
    This factorisation is possible because a1 is non-zero by definition of a polynomial.
  */

  Therefore a polynomial of the first order has one root  r1 = -a0/a1.
  And:

    a1 (x  +  a0/a1)  =  a1 (x - r1)

  shows that it can be expressed in terms of this root.


  //  Order 2 "quadratic"

  a2 x²  +  a1 x  +  a0  =  0    <=>    a2 (x²  +  (a1/a2) x  +  a0/a2)  =  0

  /*
    This factorisation is possible because a2 is non-zero by definition of a polynomial.
  */

  <=>    x²  +  (a1/a2) x  +  a0/a2  =  0    <=>    x²  +  (a1/a2) x  =  -a0/a2

  Pause. Let's consider the general case:

    (α + β)²  =  (α + β)(α + β)  =  αα +  αβ + βα + ββ  =  α² + 2αβ + β²

  Now  x²  +  (a1/a2) x  has the form:  α² + 2αβ  where:

    α = x    and    a1/a2  =  2β    so    β  =  a1 / (2 a2)

  Then  β²  =  a1² / (2 a2)²

  We can add any term we want to the equation, as long as it happens on both sides, so:

    x²  +  (a1/a2) x  +  a1² / (2 a2)²  =  a1² / (2 a2)²  -  a0/a2

    <=>
                                                            a1²  -  2² a2 a0
    ( x  +  a1 / (2 a2) )²  =  a1² / (2 a2)²  -  a0/a2  =  ------------------
                                                                 (2 a2)²
    <=>                      ___________________
                            ⎟  a1²  -  4 a2 a0         √( a1²  -  4 a2 a0 )
    x  +  a1 / (2 a2)  =  ± ⎟ -----------------  =  ± ----------------------
                            ⎷      (2 a2)²                    2 a2
    
    <=>
             √( a1²  -  4 a2 a0 )        a1        -a1  ±  √( a1²  -  4 a2 a0 )
    x  =  ± ----------------------  -  ------  =  ------------------------------
                     2 a2               2 a2                   2 a2

  a1²  -  4 a2 a0  is called the polynomial's discriminant. (Hence "discriminating" meaning "finding the roots".)
  It's usually notated Δ (upper-case delta).

  /*
    My notation is not standard (well it both is and is not, but you'll see that I'm not the inconsistent one here).
    Expressing polynomials' coefficients with indexes of "a" or "b" is standard (the latter usually reserved for denominators).

    But when dealing with the special cases of affine or quadratic, the classical notation is (respectively):

      ax + b  and  ax² + bx + c

    I don't like this. You get a1 = a, then a1 = b, etc. It's confusing for no reason.
    Plus it complies poorly with generalisation: if you keep raising the order you'll get a0 = d (as in df/dx...),
    then a0 = e (as in exp (1)...), then a0 = f (as in ƒ (x)...); orders 8 and 9 will give i and j (as in √(-1)...);
    order 23 gives a0 = x...
    Order 26 starts using Greek letters. At some point you'll run out of alphabets. You'll never run out of numbers.
    So pick one letter, give it indexes and stick with it. Or, as usual, go play with fire.

    Anyway, don't be surprised if you come across:  Δ = b² - 4ac.

    https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quadratic_formula
  */

  Therefore a polynomial of the second order has two roots:

            -a1  +  √(Δ)                    -a1  -  √(Δ)
    r1  =  --------------    and    r2  =  --------------
                2 a2                            2 a2

  Before moving further: we're still talking about filters -- even though from a distance.
  We might play around with complex numbers on our notebooks and GitHub repositories, but an analog voltage, or its digital counterpart,
  need to remain purely real.
  So I'm assuming real polynomials, starting now.
  What comes next might apply to complex polynomials, but I haven't checked.
  And I don't want to write something false because of too general phrasing.
  In short: from now on:    Im (a2)  =  Im (a1)  =  Im (a0)  =  0

  Notice that if Δ is real and negative, √(Δ) is imaginary.
  Then r1 and r2 are complex numbers and:

    Re (r1)  =  Re (r2)  =  -a1 / (2 a2)
    Im (r1)  =  √(Δ) / (2 a2)
    Im (r2)  = -√(Δ) / (2 a2)  =  -Im (r1)

  Then r1 and r2 are complex conjugates.
  If Δ is real and positive, √(Δ) is real and so are r1 and r2.

  So the roots of a real quadratic polynomial are either both real or complex conjugates.

  Now if x = r1,  x - r1  =  0  and if x = r2,  x - r2  =  0.
  So if x equals r1 OR r2:

    (x - r1)(x - r2)  =  0    since one of the factor is necessarily zero.

  Then the roots of (x - r1)(x - r2) are r1 and r2.
  Same as  x²  +  (a1/a2) x  +  a0/a2.

  So, intuitively, they're likely equal or proportional. Easy to find out:

                          /       -a1 + √(Δ) \   /       -a1 - √(Δ)  \
    (x - r1)(x - r2)  =  ( x  -  ------------ ) ( x  -  ------------- )
                          \          2 a2    /   \          2 a2     /

                                   -a1 - √(Δ)       -a1 + √(Δ)         ( -a1 + √(Δ) ) ( -a1 - √(Δ) )
                      =  x²  -  x ------------  -  ------------ x  +  -------------------------------
                                      2 a2             2 a2                      (2 a2)²

                                 -a1 - √(Δ) - a1 + √(Δ)         a1²  +  a1 √(Δ)  -  a1 √(Δ)  -  √(Δ)²
                      =  x²  -  ------------------------ x  +  ---------------------------------------
                                          2 a2                                 4 a2²

                                 -2 a1         a1² - Δ
                      =  x²  -  ------- x  +  ---------
                                  2 a2          4 a2²

                                               a1²  -  a1²  +  4 a2 a0         //  a1² - a1² cancels out,
                      =  x²  +  (a1/a2) x  +  -------------------------        //  then  4 a2  becomes a common factor
                                                      4 a2 a2                  //  to both the numerator and the denominator.

                      =  x²  +  (a1/a2) x  +  a0/a2

  Nice. Then  a2 (x²  +  (a1/a2) x  +  a0/a2)  =  a2 (x - r1)(x - r2).
  Therefore any second order (or quadratic) real polynomial can be expressed in terms of its roots and leading coefficient.

  Notice that:

    a2 (x - r1)(x - r2)  =  √(a2) (x - r1) * √(a2) (x - r2)

  and that  √(a2) (x - r1)  and  √(a2) (x - r2)  are two first-order polynomials whose respective roots are r1 and r2.

  /*  WARNING: FUNDAMENTAL CONCEPT AHEAD  */

  Therefore any second-order polynomial is the product of two first-order polynomials, whose respective roots are also
  the roots of said second-order polynomial.
  /*
    Fun example:

      x² - 1  =  0    <=>    x²  =  1    <=>    x  =  ± √(1)  =  ± 1

    So:

      x² - 1  =  (x + 1) (x - 1)

    You can check:

      3² - 1  =  9 - 1  =  8  =  4 * 2  =  (3 + 1) (3 - 1)
      7² - 1  =  49 - 1  =  48  =  8 * 6  =  (7 + 1) (7 - 1)
      10² - 1  =  100 - 1  =  99  =  11 * 9  =  (10 + 1) (10 - 1)
      0.5² - 1  =  0.25 - 1  =  -0.75  =  1.5 * -0.5  =  (0.5 + 1) (0.5 - 1)
      φ² - 1  =  φ ( φ  -  1 / φ)  =  φ ( φ  -  (φ - 1) )  =  φ (φ - φ + 1)  =  φ * 1  =  φ  =  φ² / φ  =  ( φ² ) ( 1 / φ)  =  (φ + 1) (φ - 1)

      https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_ratio

    Cool. Cool cool cool.
  */

  Let's assume that r1 and r2 are complex and define:

    P1 (x)  =  √(a2) (x - r1)  =  √(a2) ( x  -  Re (r1)  -  j Im (r1) )  =  √(a2) x  -  √(a2) Re (r1)  -  j √(a2) Im (r1)

    P2 (x)  =  √(a2) (x - r2)  =  √(a2) ( x  -  Re (r2)  -  j Im (r2) )  =  √(a2) x  -  √(a2) Re (r2)  -  j √(a2) Im (r2)

  Then:

    P1 (x) P2 (x)  =  [ √(a2) x  -  √(a2) Re (r1)  -  j √(a2) Im (r1) ] * [ √(a2) x  -  √(a2) Re (r2)  -  j √(a2) Im (r2) ]

                   =  a2 x²  -  a2 Re (r2) x  -  j a2 Im (r2) x  -  a2 Re (r1) x  +  a2 Re (r1) Re (r2)  +  j a2 Re (r1) Im (r2)
                     -  j a2 Im (r1) x  +  j a2 Im (r1) Re (r2)  -  a2 Im (r1) Im (r2)
    α2  =  a2

    α1  =  -a2 ( Re (r2)  +  j Im (r2)  +  Re (r1)  +  j Im (r1) )

    α0  =  a2 ( Re (r1) Re (r2)  +  j Re (r1) Im (r2)  +  j Im (r1) Re (r2)  -  Im (r1) Im (r2) )

    Im (α1)  =  -a2 ( Im (r2)  +  Im (r1) )

    Im (α0)  =  a2 ( Re (r1) Im (r2)  +  Im (r1) Re (r2) )

  For  P1 (x) P2 (x)  to be a real polynomial, we need all three coefficients to be purely real.
  That is:

    Im (α1)  =  0    <=>    Im (r2)  +  Im (r1)  =  0    <=>    Im (r2)  =  - Im (r1)

    Im (α0)  =  0    <=>    Re (r1) Im (r2)  +  Im (r1) Re (r2)  =  0    <=>    Re (r1) Im (r2)  =  - Im (r1) Re (r2)    <=>    Re (r1) Im (r2)  =  Re (r2) Im (r2)

    <=>    Re (r1)  =  Re (r2)        //    We got this result after dividing both sides by Im (r2), so it is only valid for non-zero imaginary parts for r1 and r2.

  What if  Im (r2)  =  0  then?

    P1 * P2  =  a2 ( x²  -  Re (r2) x  -  Re (r1) x  +  Re (r1) Re (r2)  -  j Im (r1) x  +  j Im (r1) Re (r2) )

    Im ( P1 * P2 )  =  a2 ( Im (r1) Re (r2)  -  Im (r1) x )

    Im ( P1 * P2 )  =  0    <=>    Im (r1) Re (r2)  -  Im (r1) x  =  0  ∀ x    <=>    Im (r1) Re (r2)  =  Im (r1) x  ∀ x

  Re (r2) is a constant, x is a variable. So the only solution for all x is  Im (r1)  =  0.

  Ok! We now have an "if and only if" (or "iff" for friends and family).
  So, any real second-orer polynomial can be expressed as a product of first-order polynomials, either purely real or complex conjugates.
  AND, any product of two complex conjugate, or purely real, first-order polynomials is a real second-order polynomial.
  There wouldn't be a chapter VII if not for this tiny little "and only if".
  /*
    What if...

      r1 = r2 = 0 ?

    Then:    P1 (x) P2 (x)  =  a2 (x - 0) (x - 0)  =  a2 x²

    Since  +0 = -0, any purely real number is its own complex conjugate.
    So this example satisfies the case  r2  =  conj (r1).
    But it's also a special case of r1 = r2. Let's generalise that:

      P1 (x) P2 (x)  =  a2 (x - r1) (x - r1)  =  a2 ( x²  -  2 r1 x  +  r1² )  =  a2 x²  +  2 a2 r1 x  +  a2 r1²

    which is a valid second-order polynomial.
    So when we say that order 2 => 2 roots, we do carefully not say "distinct" roots...

    Also: 1st-order polynomials which are not exactly conjugates just because of their leading coefficients can be re-arranged in proper conjugates.
    E.g.:

      8 (x - 11 + 6j) 2 (x - 11 - 6j)  =  16 (x - 11 + 6j) (x - 11 - 6j)  =  4 (x - 11 + 6j) 4 (x - 11 - 6j)

    More generally:

      a1 ( x  -  Re (r)  -  j Im (r) ) b1 ( x  -  Re (r)  +  j Im (r) )  =  √(a1 b1) ( x  -  Re (r)  -  j Im (r) ) √(a1 b1) ( x  -  Re (r)  +  j Im (r) )
  */

  //  Order N

  Cubic, quartic, quintic, sextic, heptic, octic, nonic, decic, hendecic, duodecic, tridecic... name your price.

  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Degree_of_a_polynomial#Names_of_polynomials_by_degree

