    P1 (x)  =  a1 (x - r1)        P2 (x)  =  a2 (x - r2) (x - r3)

    Π (x)  =  P1 * P2  =  a1 a2 (x - r1) (x - r2) (x - r3)    //  This expression clearly has three roots:  r1, r2 and r3.

    Π (x)  =  a1 a2 ( x²  -  r2 x  -  r1 x  +  r1 r2 ) (x - r3)  =  a1 a2 ( x³  -  r3 x²  -  r2 x²  +  r2 r3 x  -  r1 x²  +  r1 r3 x  + r1 r2 x  -  r1 r2 r3 )

           =  a1 a2 ( x³  -  (r3 + r2 + r1) x²  +  (r2 r3  +  r1 r3  +  r1 r2) x  -  r1 r2 r3 )

           =  a1 a2 x³  -  a1 a2 (r3 + r2 + r1) x²  +  a1 a2 (r2 r3  +  r1 r3  +  r1 r2) x  -  a1 a2 r1 r2 r3

    α3  =  a1 a2    &&    α2  =  - a1 a2 (r3 + r2 + r1)    &&    α1  =  a1 a2 (r2 r3  +  r1 r3  +  r1 r2)    &&    α0  =  - a1 a2 r1 r2 r3

    =>    Π (x)  =  α3 x³  +  α2 x²  +  α1 x  +  α0

  So  Π (x)  is a third-order polynomial.
  Is it real? -- Two cases:
    (1) r2 and r3 are real, then yes it is: the only way to get an imaginary number from a real one is with a negative sign and a fractional exponent.
    (2) r2 and r3 are complex conjugates...

  Let's try (2):

    α2  =  - a1 a2 ( Re (r2)  -  j Im (r2)  +  Re (r2)  +  j Im (r2)  +  r1 )  =  - a1 a2 ( 2 Re (r2)  +  r1 )    //  ok.

    α1  =  a1 a2 [ ( Re (r2)  +  j Im (r2) ) ( Re (r2)  -  j Im (r2) )  +  r1 Re (r2)  -  j r1 Im (r2)  +  r1 Re (r2)  +  j r1 Im (r2) ]

        =  a1 a2 [ Re (r2)²  -  j Re (r2) Im (r2)  +  j Re (r2) Im (r2)  +  Im (r2)²  +  2 r1 Re (r2) ]

        =  a1 a2 [ Re (r2)²  +  Im (r2)²  +  2 r1 Re (r2) ]    //  ok.

    α0  =  - a1 a2 [ r1 ( Re (r2)  +  j Im (r2) ) ( Re (r2)  -  j Im (r2) ) ]  =  - a1 a2 r1 ( Re (r2)²  -  j Re (r2) Im (r2)  +  j Im (r2) Re (r2)  +  Im (r2)² )

        =  - a1 a2 r1 [ Re (r2)²  +  Im (r2)² ]    //  ok.

  Not bad, we have "if" -- but not "iff".
  In syllogistical prose:

    The product of any real 1st-order polynomial with any real 2nd-order polynomial is a real 3rd-order polynomial. (universal affirmative)

  Which converts to:

    A real 3rd-order polynomial CAN be the product of a real 1st-order and a real 2dn-order polynomial. (particular affirmative)

  Not satisfying. We need grown-up maths.



